+/// (0xa37c in 8;7;3) has exactly two entries -- 0x00740000 for a 256 KB
+/// machine and 0x00800000 for a 1 MB one -- and bootrom.s indexes it with a
+/// memory-size index the self-test can only ever set to 0 or 1. The firmware
+/// is structurally incapable of handing out an address above 0x7FFFFF, so
+/// every documented structure keeps its documented address, Free_RAM keeps its
+/// full extent, and nothing can ever be allocated on top of the screen.
